## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses the ability to design robust distributed signal processing algorithms that can operate under real-world constraints such as asynchrony, packet loss, and computational limitations across edge devices.

## Key Concepts
- Distributed whitening and demixing across multiple nodes
- Gossip-based consensus for matrix fusion
- Time-varying mixing matrix tracking with adaptive algorithms
- Partial observation handling and permutation alignment
- Real-time performance evaluation using SDR/SIR metrics and latency constraints

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code sketch for a single gossip round
function gossipRound(W_local, neighbors) {
  const W_agg = average([W_local, ...neighbors.map(n => n.W_local)]);
  return normalize(W_agg);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests privacy-aware distributed blind source separation, a realistic constraint in smart rooms. It probes understanding of distributed ICA, whitening, and robust updates under communication limits and node outages.

## Key Concepts
- Distributed ICA with local whitening and demixing
- Privacy-preserving statistics exchange (secure aggregation)
- Asynchronous synchronization and latency budgeting
- Missing data resilience and fault tolerance

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: compute local whitening (illustrative only)
function localWhiten(X) {
  // X: channels x samples
  const cov = X.dot(X.transpose());
  // eigen-decomposition placeholder
  const { eigenVectors: V, eigenValues: D } = eigenDecompose(cov);
  const DinvSqrt = diagonalMatrix(D.map(v => 1 / Math.sqrt(v)));
  return DinvSqrt.multiply(V.transpose()).multiply(X);
}
```

